# Line endings are a property of the repository, not of the machine that
# checked it out.
#
# Git converts to CRLF on checkout wherever core.autocrlf says so, which
# on Windows is the installer's default. The structural guards read the
# sources as text and anchor to the end of a line, so a carriage return
# there does not merely look untidy: `$` stops matching, a sed range runs
# past its close to the end of the file, and a guard reports about a
# fraction of the tree while printing the same SUCCESS it prints for all
# of it. One of them was found green over a planted violation with five
# sixths of its population silently gone.
#
# So the working tree is LF, everywhere, whatever the platform. A shell
# script with a carriage return in it is a syntax error to every shell
# that is not Windows's, PostScript sources are read line-wise by both
# the interpreter and the guards, and the registers are compared as
# sorted sets of whole lines.
* text=auto eol=lf

# What is not text, and must not be touched at all.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.sha256 text eol=lf

# The Visual Studio project files are stored as Visual Studio writes
# them, CRLF and a byte-order mark, and are left exactly as they are:
# nothing here reads them, and normalising them would rewrite every one.
*.sln -text
*.vcxproj -text
*.vcxproj.filters -text
visual_studio/config.h -text

# Written by a drawing program and stored as it wrote it.
doc/stack.eps -text
